# Web app standards (`apps/web`)

Adapt paths and commands to your repo. Load
`.agents/skills/react-next-performance/SKILL.md` before writing or refactoring
React/Next UI.

## TypeScript & clean code

- Strict TypeScript; avoid `any` unless justified and documented
- Typed API payloads and component props; no type laundering (`as any`,
  widen-then-assert)
- Small modules, single responsibility; shared contracts live in
  `packages/shared` (one source of truth)
- Prefer `@/` imports; no deep `../../../` chains

## Folder structure

- `app/` — App Router; thin pages that compose features
- `components/ui/` — design-system primitives only
- `components/<feature>/` — feature UI
- `features/` — hooks, mutations, selectors (not JSX dumps)
- `lib/` — env, API clients, domain utilities
- `app/api/` — BFF only; no domain business logic in the framework layer

## Performance

- Server Components by default; tight `"use client"` boundaries
- `next/dynamic` for heavy client islands (canvas, editors, playgrounds)
- `React.memo` on custom canvas nodes; stable `nodeTypes`/`edgeTypes`
  outside render
- `useMemo` / `useCallback` on hot paths (handlers, derived selectors) — not
  cargo-culted
- Prefer derived state and event handlers over `useEffect` chains
- No effect-driven data fetching when the server layer can own it
- Never call your own route handlers from Server Components

## Production checklist

- Layouts, `loading.tsx` / `error.tsx`, streaming where useful
- Parallel data fetching; env hygiene (`NEXT_PUBLIC_*` only for public values)
- No dep-less effects, no fresh-identity deps, no per-token `setState`
  (see `/AGENTS.md` hard rules)
